      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the most abundant and essential substance on Earth, uniquely suited to support life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, based on its physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The \"Weird\" Science of Water)\nWater behaves differently than almost any other substance on Earth, and these unique properties are what make it so special:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter: **solid** (ice), **liquid** (water), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **Universal Solvent:** Water is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how water transports vital nutrients and minerals through our bodies and through ecosystems.\n* **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps our body temperatures stable.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n* Only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. \n* Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use (in lakes, rivers, and shallow groundwater).\n* Water is constantly moving in a continuous cycle called the **water cycle** (or hydrological cycle), evaporating from the oceans, forming clouds, falling as rain or snow, and flowing back into the sea.\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nLife as we know it"
